{"number": 124, "text": "      59\n\n\n                     When    things   go wrong\n                     Many  idioms with fall refer to situations where things go wrong, people encounter problems,\n                     or where something  fails. Read these news cuttings and note the idioms.\n\n                     The  minister of education seems to have fallen\n                     from grace’. There are rumours that the president\n                     is planning to sack her next week when ...\n\n\n                        Occasionally,  secret documents    fall into\n                        the  wrong  hands’  and  national security is\n                        threatened. One such case has raised fears ...\n\n\n                     Last year, a petition with 10,000 signatures |\n                     demanding   an end to the tax fell on\n                     ears’, so           protesters tok  to the\n                     streets and ..\n\n                                                                             The global banking  system  seemed   to be\n                           House prices have fallen through the\n                                                                             falling apart at the seams’ in 2009 ...\n                           last six months, leaving.\n                                 how\n\n                      done something which made people in            5 broke a law or regulation\n                      authority stop liking or admiring them           did not come up to the standard of\n                     the  wrong people obtain and control them             something which seemed like a good\n                     3 was ignored                                     idea but was not wise\n                     * dropped to a very low level                   8 be in a very bad state and about to fail\n\n |             B     Other   idioms   with   fall\n                     People were  falling over themselves to volunteer as helpers for the president’s visit. [very\n                     keen and eager]\n                     She never seems  to have to work to get what she wants. Everything just seems to fall / drop\n                     into her lap. [happen or be given to her without any effort on her part]\n                     Fabio seems  to have fallen for Elena hook, line and sinker! [fallen madly in love with]\n                     My colleagues at the office played a joke on me, and | fell for it hook, line and sinker.\n                     [completely believed something which was not true]\n                     I’m afraid you will have to fall into line and accept the company’s rules and policy. [start to\n                     accept the rules of an organisation]\n                     Here are two proverbs which  include fall:\n                     Pride comes  before a fall. [if someone is too confident or arrogant, something bad will happen,\n                     which will show that they are not as good as they think they are]\n                     The bigger they  are / come, the harder they fall. [the more power or success someone has, the\n                     harder it will be for them to lose it]\n                     See Unit 11 for more  proverbs.\n\n\n                     English Idioms in Use Advanced\n      122", "char_count": 3263, "mean_conf": 92.14, "word_count": 405, "words": [{"text": "122", "x0": 125, "y0": 3360, "x1": 187, "y1": 3389, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "Many", "x0": 428, "y0": 451, "x1": 528, "y1": 491, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "idioms", "x0": 541, "y0": 449, "x1": 668, "y1": 483, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "with", "x0": 681, "y0": 448, "x1": 762, "y1": 483, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "fall", "x0": 777, "y0": 448, "x1": 836, "y1": 483, "conf": 94, "bold": false}, {"text": "refer", "x0": 849, "y0": 448, "x1": 937, "y1": 483, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "to", "x0": 948, "y0": 453, "x1": 985, "y